  uh and at the time obviously the the  narrative was very  simple about you know um that there was  either  um you know a flu-like thing with a  cough and a temperature or  um you know if you were clinically  vulnerable or old that you would end up  you know in hospital possibly uh being  ventilated  um so when we got this you know i got  ill with a range of  symptoms that were a bit like a cold and  my  daughter had slightly different symptoms  my son had different symptoms again  initially you know i didn't really put  two and two together  um and it was only when i lost my sense  of smell and taste and at the time that  wasn't a known  symptom um but when i googled it it said  that in iran  at the time of the pandemic it was the  most um  the most searched for term in google and  i thought okay  putting two and two together i think  this must be coronavirus  um and i went for when i tried to speak  to the doctors about it they said oh no  that's not a symptom  so it was very it was very difficult at  the time to get people to actually  believe that that was  that that was what it was and of course  everybody  was googling back then because we knew  so little about it  so when did you realize that uh you  might actually suffer from  what we now know as long covid  so i've for me um i just didn't didn't  seem to make a recovery  um so there wasn't particularly a time i  think after about six weeks i had a  brief recovery  uh and then i was ill again but it felt  very um  you know i describe it's a bit like a  roller coaster of  different symptoms coming and going  and uh and sort of occasional  day better days and then worst days it  just felt like i wasn't really getting  better  and and it felt such a um an unknown  kind of uh illness because of all these  different symptoms  my daughter was really poorly for six  weeks  um and then she  didn't really get better she kind of  continued with fatigue  um you know nausea and other symptoms uh  yeah we just saw  we just saw we just saw pictures of your  daughter and your son just now as you  were telling us  would you say that what we now know is  long covid  uh that it manifests itself differently  with children than with  adults well we've done some research  uh with long covered kids um and we've  sort of established that actually a lot  of the symptoms are very similar  but children do tend to have  an increased amount of gastric issues  quite often  we have found children um ending up with  you know appendicitis you know or  peritonitis  we see quite a lot of the hymns like  symptoms you know  the pediatric inflammatory multi-system  disorder we see sort of similar symptoms  to that  but we're also seeing um  neuropsychiatric symptoms you know so  things like  uh ticks tourette's uh seizures  uh so there's a real gamut of different  symptoms in  children kind of manifest slightly  differently um so there's a lot of the  same  symptoms as adults and there are certain  features that seem to be particularly  key in the pediatric cohort there is of  course still very little known  about long coverage even with adults uh  and that is probably  why uh you co-founded a campaign  known as long cove with kids what are  you hoping to achieve  uh with this campaign uh well  well it's both a campaign group and a  support group um  so i joined forces with uh sami  mcfarland who is the founder  and we joined forces in october because  um you know we were part of different  support groups and campaign groups for  the adult  community uh and and realized that there  was there was just a lack of anything  for children  um and the children narrative was just  being left out completely  of all discussions so we came together  really to to provide a group  for parents because it's a really  difficult place to be  um quite often the parents are ill  themselves and to have children who are  ill and  that that illness to be being denied or  for the parents sometimes to feel like  they're being um  gaslighted or so it was really just to  allow people to come in  and find other people and and then from  that we started to say well okay  we need to start getting this message  out that we need people to know  because we are not going to see a change  in the support for our children unless  we actually  uh raise awareness of this so that's  that was enough  so francis uh you and your children  would you say you have fully recovered  now  um it's difficult to say i'm much better  um the last really i mean i i was really  ill until about  september so from march to september  and then i uh and then i took some time  off work i  rested completely now i  have a relapse um probably twice a month  uh of a few days and i have to go you  know take myself back to bed and i have  to rest  um but i'm quite aware of of you know  when um right and but my children  uh no they're not better and they they  have relapses whenever they  do anything that exerts them um they  went back to school  uh the last couple of weeks to see how  they'd get on  and they've been ill since they since  they finished right  well francis simpson uh thank you so  much uh for sharing your experience with  us all the best to you and your kids of  course  thanks for being on the program thank  you  well last year no one dared to hope that  we'd have a vaccine  so soon and while most of us embrace  this fantastic achievement  we also wonder if there could be any  unwanted side effects  it's time for your questions now here's  our science correspondent derek williams  could a vaccine cause a positive pcr  result  just a quick reminder first that that  that pcr  stands for polymerase chain reaction  and and tests that employ the technology  are considered  the gold standard for for detecting  the viral genome in swab samples  now because some vaccines use  information from that genome  could they also trigger a positive  result  um this question comes up all the time  and and the answer is  is no believe me if it did then  you would have heard about it because  millions of vaccinated people  would have suddenly started testing  positive for covet 19  after getting shots there would there  would be no way to distinguish between  them  and and asymptomatic carriers it'd be  chaos  but but nothing even remotely like that  has happened  now if you get back a positive pcr  result it's almost certainly  because you've been infected even though  you might have coincidentally  received a vaccine a day or two  beforehand  don't forget that it takes your body  around  two weeks to build up a significant  immune response after you've received  the first dose of a vaccine  and in that time people do catch  covid19 quite a few i i get email from  them all the time  the science behind why vaccines can't  cause  a positive pcr result is fairly  complicated but basically  boils down to the fact that none of the  currently approved vaccines  use a full version of the real virus  to to kick-start an immune response  instead  they're based on short specific  stretches  from the sars cov2 genome that that  actually  degrade pretty quickly in cells they're  not detected  by pcr only replicating  viable viruses so if you test  positive it's time to talk to a medical  professional  the vaccine is not to blame  [Music]  and eric williams will be back to answer  more of your questions again tomorrow  before we go and this is not an april  fool's joke  if you are anywhere with a mask  requirement you will know the problem  every time you want to eat or take a sip  of your drink you have to remove it  potentially putting yourself and others  at risk  well here's a possible solution the nose  only mask  it allows you to take a bite while  maintaining at least some level of  protection but zero  dignity it's been invented by  researchers in mexico where street food  is particularly popular although it's  better than nothing the who points out  that a mask that covers the nose  mouth and chin 